    id: "mc-04-03-96",
    title: "MC 4-3-96 - Operation of Spread Spectrum Technology Radios",
    shortTitle: "Operation of Spread Spectrum Technology Radios",
    type: "regulation",
    category: "Memorandum Circulars",
    status: "amended",
    effective_date: "1996-03-04",
    tags: ["spread-spectrum", "spectrum-allocation"],
    cross_references: ["mc-07-06-96"],
    fullText: "MEMORANDUM CIRCULAR\n                                  NO. 4-3-96\n\nSUBJECT: Operation of Spread Spectrum Technology Radios within the bands\n         915-928 Mhz, 2400-2483.5 Mhz and 5725-5850.0Mhz.\n\n         Pursuant to Act as amended, the existing radio laws and to address the\nrequirement of radio communication services using Spread Spectrum Technology, the\nfrequency bands 915-928MHz may be used on a secondary, non-priority and non-\ninterference basis.\n\n       The output power of each station shall not exceed one (1) watt effective radiated\npower (ERP). Operation of these radios are subject to special licensing guidelines.\n\n        This circular shall take effect immediately and it supersedes any circular, order of\ninstruction or part thereof which is inconsistent herewith.\n\n       Quezon City, Philippines, 4th day of March 1996.\n\n                                                             SIMEON L. KINTANAR\n                                                               Commissioner",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Authorized Bands and Power Limit", body: "The 915-928 MHz band (among others) may be used for Spread Spectrum Technology on a secondary, non-priority, non-interference basis, with station output power capped at one watt effective radiated power (ERP). Operation is subject to special licensing guidelines. Effective immediately, superseding inconsistent prior issuances." }
    ]
